s": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003"], "suggestions": ["Repair both upstream supply modules before combining them.", "Separate HCN-derived glycine-precursor chemistry from free-glycine production.", "Provide an explicit environmental transfer or wet-dry cycle connecting cyanide chemistry to the acidic condensation step."]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_011": 0.18}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.18, "pathway_coherence": 0.33, "environmental_progression": 0.22, "target_achievement": 0.3, "overall_confidence": 0.25, "feedback": "The literature supports threonine after hydrolysis of irradiated HCN/cyanide products, not a one-step HCN + NH3 -> threonine transformation. rxn_011 is unbalanced, lacks the ionizing-radiation driver, and skips the oligomeric or polymeric precursor or residue that actually stores the threonine skeleton. It should be modeled as an ensemble route, not an elementary step.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_011"], "suggestions": ["Introduce an irradiated HCN or cyanide residue node.", "Add ionizing radiation as the energy source and separate analytical hydrolysis from environmental synthesis.", "Do not represent this route as a single balanced reaction to free threonine."]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_012": 0.24}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.24, "pathway_coherence": 0.4, "environmental_progression": 0.28, "target_achievement": 0.43, "overall_confidence": 0.33, "feedback": "Parker et al. provide real product-level evidence that H2S-rich spark discharge can generate threonine, but rxn_012 is not a chemically valid elementary or net stoichiometric reaction as written. It omits water, the electrical discharge, the atmospheric plus aqueous apparatus context, and the unresolved multistep precursor network. This route is better represented as an ensemble observation than as a direct gas-to-threonine conversion.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_012"], "suggestions": ["Represent the Miller system as an