m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Threonine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Surface-dominated, acetaldehyde-centered network. Three experimentally grounded threonine families are represented: (i) glycine and oligoglycine capture of acetaldehyde in warm acidic waters, (ii) the cyanosulfidic sequence acetaldehyde -> lactonitrile -> lactaldehyde -> threonine aminonitrile -> threonine, and (iii) aminomalononitrile capture of acetaldehyde followed by hydrolysis/decarboxylation. Alternative Cu-assisted versus Cu-free photoredox and neutral versus acidic aminonitrile hydrolysis are separated as distinct branches. To keep the graph conservation-valid, unresolved radiolysis, discharge, and ice-residue routes are not encoded as single black-box edges, and composite workup nodes are written with explicit water, ammonia, and CO2 bookkeeping. Hot hydrothermal production is not favored because the target-specific literature is stronger for surface and concentrated-pool chemistry.\n\nMOLECULES (15 nodes):\n  - mol_001: HCN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_003: H2O (water) [starting]\n  - mol_004: C2H5NO2 (glycine) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_005: C4H8N2O3 (glycylglycine) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_006: C6H11N3O4 (triglycine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_007: C2H4O